Title:
The EDR Bypass Roadmap: Technical Evolution and API Subversion

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post presents a timeline-style roadmap of how Windows EDR evasion techniques evolved from 2010 through 2026, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK and anchored in telemetry/visibility gaps. It explains the offensive progression from classic injection (DLL injection, hollowing, manual mapping) to defeating userland API hooks (hook detection/unhooking), then bypassing `ntdll` via direct syscalls and later restoring “legitimate” context with indirect syscalls and call-stack spoofing. It covers the shift in defender telemetry toward ETW and attacker responses such as `EtwEventWrite` patching, AMSI patching, and ETW session tampering, while noting ETW-TI’s kernel-mode resilience. Modern tradecraft highlighted includes Early Bird/Early Cascade APC injection, threadless approaches, sleep obfuscation (Ekko/Foliage-style), and memory evasion like module stomping and transacted/phantom DLL hollowing. The final phase focuses on kernel/hardware-assisted evasion (BYOVD, kernel callback removal, PPL stripping, hardware-breakpoint-driven syscalls) and frames HVCI + Microsoft’s vulnerable driver blocklist as key defensive constraints. It’s useful for red teams/pentesters building Windows implants and for blue teams/detection engineers understanding which telemetry layers are being targeted and why.

Technical Focus:
- Userland API hooking (inline/IAT) detection and unhooking
- Direct vs indirect syscalls (SSN resolution: Hell’s Gate/Halos Gate/Tartarus Gate)
- Call stack integrity evasion (ROP gadgets, return address spoofing, stack pivoting)
- ETW/AMSI tampering and ETW-TI limitations
- Advanced injection primitives (APC/Early Bird, thread hijacking, VEH-based execution)
- Kernel/hardware evasion (BYOVD, kernel notify routine removal, PPL bypass, DR0–DR3 breakpoints)

Use Cases:
- Build or assess Windows post-exploitation loaders/injection chains against modern EDRs
- Create detection hypotheses for hook tampering, syscall abuse, ETW patching, and stack spoofing
- Evaluate exposure to BYOVD and prioritize HVCI/vulnerable-driver-blocklist enforcement
- Map offensive techniques to ATT&CK for purple-team exercises and telemetry gap analysis
- Guide research into memory-scanning bypasses (module stomping, transacted hollowing) and countermeasures

Keywords:
EDR evasion, Windows internals, API hooking, inline hook, IAT patching, ntdll unhooking, direct syscalls, indirect syscalls, Hell’s Gate, Halos Gate, Tartarus Gate, SysWhispers, call stack spoofing, ROP gadget, ETW, EtwEventWrite patch, ETW-TI, AMSI bypass, Early Bird APC injection, LdrInitializeThunk, thread hijacking, VEH, sleep obfuscation, Ekko, BYOVD, vulnerable driver, IOCTL, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, PspCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, PPL bypass, EPROCESS.Protection, HVCI, module stomping, transacted hollowing, NtCreateTransaction, NtRollbackTransaction, hardware breakpoints, DR0-DR3